~20% of AKS pods never received a device ID from the server because the initial `"device need device_id"` request was silently dropped. Two back-to-back `getAsyncRemote().sendText()` calls in `onOpen()` raced — if the first async send was still in-flight, the second threw `IllegalStateException` per JSR-356, which was swallowed, and the server never saw the request.

## Primary fix — `sendMessage()` (`SimulatorWSClient`)

Replaced non-blocking `getAsyncRemote()` with synchronous `getBasicRemote()` and made the method `synchronized` to serialize all callers (WebSocket callback thread + keepalive scheduler thread):

```java
// Before — second send races with the first async send still in-flight
session.getAsyncRemote().sendText(json);  // throws IllegalStateException ~20% of the time

// After — blocks until sent; synchronized prevents concurrent callers
public synchronized void sendMessage(...) {
    Session s = this.session;
    if (s == null || !s.isOpen()) {
        System.out.println("[sendMessage] Skipped: session is " + ...);
        return;
    }
    s.getBasicRemote().sendText(json);
}
```

Also added an explicit null/closed-session guard with a diagnostic log so dropped messages are visible rather than silent.

## Visibility fixes — `volatile` on cross-thread fields

Fields written by the WebSocket callback thread and read by the worker thread (or keepalive scheduler) were missing `volatile`, leaving the JVM free to cache stale values indefinitely.

**`SimulatorWSClient`**
- `session` — read by keepalive scheduler thread
- `iotWorkerFuture` — static field shared across callback invocations

**`IotClient`**
- `isReadytoRun` — polled in the worker thread's busy-loop; without `volatile` the worker may never see `setReadytoRun(true)`
- `DEVICE_ID`, `IOTHUB_DEVICE_CONNECTION_STRING` — written on `device.config.update`; read when building telemetry payloads
- `INITIAL_RETRY_DELAY_SECONDS`, `MAX_RETRY_DELAY_SECONDS`, `MAX_RETRIES`, `MESSAGE_INTERVAL_SECONDS` — same cross-thread visibility problem

